;; Clojure namespaces a `.cljc` module can keep requiring after it moves to
;; Kotoba, and -- as loudly as what is provided -- what is deliberately NOT
;; here.
;;
;; A shim is only worth having if it is exact. Kotoba's string surface is
;; addressed in UTF-8 bytes (`string-length` of "aあb" is 5; `string-code-point-at`
;; takes a byte offset; a split index REFUSES rather than returning something
;; wrong -- all measured 2026-08-31). Three of `clojure.string`'s functions are
;; indifferent to that. Five more (2026-09-02) are questions about code
;; points, and Kotoba controls its own encoding: the byte width of a code
;; point is a function of the code point, so a scan can walk a string code
;; point by code point and answer exactly what the JVM answers. The rest are
;; not, and providing them approximately under their real names would answer,
;; quietly, wrong.
;;
;; Where such a function is worth having anyway, it lives here under a
;; DIFFERENT name in a `kotoba.*` namespace, saying in the name what it
;; actually answers -- `kotoba.string/byte-index-of` for the byte-offset half
;; of `clojure.string/index-of`. The absent entry then points at it, so the
;; reason and the replacement are one lookup apart. A caller reaching for the
;; Clojure name has to stop and change the call, which is the moment to notice
;; that the number means something else now.
;;
;; Consumed through the ordinary project route, which needs no JVM:
;;   amu check app.cljk --source-path <app> --source-path <this repo>/lang/compat --jvm-free
{:kotoba.lang.compat/version 1
 :kotoba.lang.compat/authority "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"
 :kotoba.lang.compat/workspace-adr
 "90-docs/adr/2608311750-cljk-migration-cost-is-not-effects.edn"

 :measured
 {:as-of "2026-09-02"
  :string-length-is :utf8-bytes
  :evidence "(string-length \"aあb\") = 5, (string-byte-length \"aあb\") = 5, (string-code-point-at \"aあb\" 1) = 12354, (string-substring \"aあb\" 1 2) refused with \"string substring index splits a UTF-8 code point\" (2026-08-31); (string-code-point-at \"ab\" 2) traps \"string code-point offset is out of bounds\", so every scan here stops at the byte length before reading (2026-09-02)"
  :oracle "clojure.string itself, called from test/kotoba/lang/clojure_string_compat_test.clj on the same inputs: 48 of 48 for the three 2026-08-31 functions (16 inputs x 3); the 2026-09-02 functions are compared on 26 whitespace-bearing inputs x 4 (blank?/trim/triml/trimr) + 14 inputs for reverse, and the whitespace predicate itself against java.lang.Character/isWhitespace for every code point below U+3100 plus every code point the JVM says is whitespace -- the counts the test prints are the record, this sentence is the shape"
  :regex-literal "a #\"...\" literal in guest source is a READER rejection (:kotoba/source-read-failed, \"source reader rejected input\"), not a value -- measured 2026-09-02, amu bb51dc14. So a string-only split/replace could not silently accept a pattern; that arity is refused before any function is called. It is not the reason those stay absent (see :absent split)."
  :list-of-string "[:list :string] admits construction (typed-list-new) and vector-count only. vector-at / vector-conj on it are refused with \"expression type mismatch: expected vector-i64, got [:list :string]\"; first / count with \"operation has no admitted lowering\" / a type mismatch. Measured 2026-09-02, amu bb51dc14. A list of strings can be built and counted and nothing can be read back out of it."
  :option-return-on-wasm32 "a function whose result is :option-i64 with (option-some x) and (option-none) on the two arms of an if compiles on the KIR backend and refuses wasm32 AND wasm32-browser (\"unsupported typed Wasm expression\", exit 70) -- and it refuses the whole project even when main never reaches it (two-module reproduction, 2026-09-02, amu bb51dc14). This is why nothing here returns an option."}

 :modules
 {:clojure.string
  {:path "lang/compat/clojure/string.kotoba"
   :provides #{starts-with? ends-with? includes?
               blank? trim triml trimr reverse}
   :equivalence :exact
   :added {:as-of "2026-09-02"
           :names #{blank? trim triml trimr reverse}
           :whitespace "java.lang.Character.isWhitespace (JDK 21, Unicode 15) written out as a bounded predicate over code points: U+0009..U+000D, U+001C..U+001F, U+0020, U+1680, U+2000..U+2006, U+2008..U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+205F, U+3000. Deliberately NOT in it: U+00A0, U+2007, U+202F (non-breaking, which Java excludes) and U+0085 (NEL, which Java never counted). The oracle sweep is what holds that table to the JVM; if a JDK moves the set the sweep goes red and the table follows."
           :reverse "java.lang.StringBuilder.reverse keeps surrogate pairs together, so clojure.string/reverse is a code-point reversal and this one agrees on astral input; asserted with U+1F600 in the oracle, not assumed."}
   :costs {:ends-with? "walks the haystack once by code point to find whether the suffix begins on a boundary; recursion here is fuel-bounded like any other guest recursion"
           :trim "two walks (left edge, then right edge from there); triml and trimr one each; blank? one, stopping at the first non-whitespace code point"
           :reverse "one walk with one string-concat and one string-substring per code point"}
   :hazards
   {:substring-prefix "(string-substring \"あ\" 0 1) refuses, so a substring-based starts-with? TRAPS where the answer is false. Compared by code point instead."
    :empty-needle "string-contains? refuses an empty needle (empty-string-search-needle) where clojure.string answers true, which is why includes? is not an alias."
    :arity "each provided function is the one-string arity only. clojure.string's nil-accepting behaviour (blank? nil is true) has no counterpart because there is no nil string; a caller passing a second argument gets an arity refusal at check time, never a different answer."}
   :absent
   {index-of
    {:reason "clojure.string returns a UTF-16 code-unit index and nil when absent. The index half is answered now (below); the nil half is what keeps the Clojure name out: its exact spelling is an :option-i64 result, and a function with (option-none) on one arm of an if refuses to lower to wasm32 for the WHOLE project, reached or not (:measured :option-return-on-wasm32). Under the Clojure name this module would stop compiling to wasm32 for every consumer of starts-with?."
     :what-would-be-needed "wasm32 lowering an if over option-some / option-none. Then utf16-index-of's body, returning an option, is index-of."
     :resolved "2026-09-01, byte-index-of for the byte-offset half; 2026-09-02, kotoba.string/utf16-index-of for the index Clojure actually returns (a code point above U+FFFF counts as two). The 2026-09-01 note said a character-index abstraction was absent; utf16-index-of IS one, by scan rather than by a second string representation. What is still absent is only the nil."
     :instead #{kotoba.string/utf16-index-of kotoba.string/byte-index-of}
     :measured "2026-09-02: utf16-index-of agrees with clojure.string/index-of (nil read as -1) on every oracle input in test/kotoba/lang/kotoba_string_compat_test.clj, astral cases included."}
    last-index-of
    {:reason "same nil question as index-of."
     :instead kotoba.string/utf16-last-index-of
     :measured "2026-09-02: agrees with clojure.string/last-index-of (nil read as -1) on the same inputs, including the empty needle, where Java answers the UTF-16 length."}
    lower-case
    {:reason "clojure.string case-folds over Unicode. An ASCII-only fold answers for every input and is wrong for most of them. A bounded table over code points -- the same shape as the whitespace predicate, just several hundred ranges rather than eleven -- would be exact and is possible; it is unstarted (2026-09-02), and string-fold-case is not it (its contract is fold-for-comparison, not Character/toLowerCase)."}
    upper-case {:reason "same as lower-case: possible as a bounded table, unstarted."}
    capitalize {:reason "same as lower-case, plus the first-character question; possible as a bounded table, unstarted."}
    split
    {:reason "returns a sequence of strings. The regex arity is NOT the obstacle any more -- a regex literal is a reader rejection here (:measured :regex-literal), so a string-only split could never silently take a pattern. The obstacle is the return: [:list :string] can be built and counted and nothing can be read back out of it (:measured :list-of-string), so a split that returned one would hand the caller a value with no accessor."
     :what-would-be-needed "an element accessor on [:list :string]. The scan itself is written and measured in a scratch file (a,b,,c on , counts 4 segments) and will move here when the value can be read."}
    split-lines {:reason "same return as split."}
    join
    {:reason "consumes a sequence of strings: the same [:list :string] with no accessor (:measured :list-of-string). string-join is the bounded eight-part builtin for the literal case."}
    replace
    {:reason "the regex arity is refused by the reader, as for split, so the string arity under the Clojure name would no longer be a hazard; string-replace-all exists. Unstarted 2026-09-02 -- it needs its own oracle (java.lang.String.replace on the same inputs) before it is provided, and nothing in this workspace has asked for it yet."}
    replace-first {:reason "same as replace, and string-replace-all is all occurrences."}}}

  ;; The error-carrying trio, admitted by the typed abort ability rather than
  ;; by the string surface. `lang/abort-ability.edn` slice 2.
  :clojure.core
  {:path "lang/compat/clojure/core.kotoba"
   :provides #{ex-info ex-message ex-data}
   :equivalence :document-value-not-a-throwable
   :why "The abort ability carries a `:document` as its error type E, so the
         shape a .cljc module already writes -- a message and a data map,
         thrown, caught, read back -- moves across without changing at the call
         sites. What does NOT move across is the Throwable: clojure.core/ex-info
         returns an ExceptionInfo with a class, a stack trace and a cause, and
         this returns an ordinary two-entry document. It is not an approximation
         of a Throwable; it answers the three questions the trio is used for."
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-02"
    :document-error-type "(throw (ex-info \"empty input\" (document-map ...))) gives the throwing function the interface [:result :i64 :document] and the row #{:abort}; a bare `catch` binds the document and an explicit `(catch :document e ...)` pins it. NO frontend work was needed for this -- the E was already whatever the throw's operand types as. Measured against kotoba-sema f7c56ab3."
    :propagation "the document E survives slice-2 propagation: `read2` writes no throw and no try, calls `parse`, and the catcher two calls away reads the same message back"
    :oracle "clojure.core/ex-info itself, called from test/kotoba/lang/clojure_core_compat_test.clj on the same message and data, asked the same two questions; the divergences below are asserted against it rather than described"
    :data-literal "(ex-info \"m\" {:input s}) does NOT compile -- a map literal is a constant document and `s` is a string local, refused with \"expression type mismatch: expected i64, got string\". The data argument is built with `document-map`."}
   :hazards
   {:no-nil "clojure.core answers nil for a missing message and for the data of a non-ExceptionInfo. There is no nil here: a document with no :message reads as \"\" and one with no :data as (document-null), which counts as empty. A caller testing for nil has to change the test."
    :non-map-traps "(ex-message e) and (ex-data e) TRAP with document-map-required on a document that is not a map, where clojure.core/ex-message ANSWERS for any Throwable. Nothing is silently wrong -- the program stops -- but it stops where Clojure would have returned a value."
    :arity "the one-argument (message only) and three-argument (message, data, cause) arities of ex-info are absent: a cause is another Throwable, and there is no Throwable here to be one. The two directions are NOT symmetric, measured 2026-09-02: (ex-info \"m\") is refused with \"function call arity mismatch\", but (ex-info \"m\" data cause) is ADMITTED and the third argument is dropped without a word. That is a general hole in kotoba-sema, not something about this module -- (two 1 2 3) on (defn- two [a b] (+ a b)) answers 3 -- and it is the same shape as the `if` arity defect kotoba-sema ADR 0007 records. Pinned by test/kotoba/lang/clojure_core_compat_test.clj so a fix is visible; NOT fixed here."
    :not-thrown-by-clojure "the document this builds is thrown by Kotoba's typed `throw`, which lowers to a result value. It never becomes a JVM exception, so nothing outside the guest can catch it as one."}
   :absent
   {ex-cause {:reason "a cause is a Throwable and there is none here."}
    Throwable->map {:reason "serialises a Throwable's class, message, data, cause chain and stack trace. Four of those five do not exist in this language."}}}

  ;; Not a Clojure namespace. The honest counterpart to one of the absent
  ;; names above, under a name that says which kind of index it answers.
  :kotoba.string
  {:path "lang/compat/kotoba/string.kotoba"
   :provides #{byte-index-of utf16-index-of utf16-last-index-of}
   :replaces {byte-index-of clojure.string/index-of
              utf16-index-of clojure.string/index-of
              utf16-last-index-of clojure.string/last-index-of}
   :equivalence {byte-index-of :byte-offset-not-character-index
                 utf16-index-of :clojure-index-with-minus-one-for-nil
                 utf16-last-index-of :clojure-index-with-minus-one-for-nil}
   :measured
   {:as-of "2026-09-01"
    :oracle "a naive search over (.getBytes s \"UTF-8\") written independently of the Kotoba source, in test/kotoba/lang/kotoba_string_compat_test.clj; 28 inputs, all agreeing, 18 of them with a multi-byte haystack"
    :divergence "(byte-index-of \"aあb\" \"b\") = 4 where (clojure.string/index-of \"aあb\" \"b\") = 2; asserted, not assumed, so the argument for the name cannot quietly evaporate"
    :route "amu check <entry> --source-path <app> --source-path lang/compat --jvm-free, exit 0, module-order [kotoba.string app]"
    :executed "not only lowered: compiled to wasm32 (2,279 bytes, exit 0, JVM-free), instantiated through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs, main() = 4n for (byte-index-of \"a\u3042b\" \"b\") and 3n for (byte-index-of \"\u3042a\" \"a\"). wasm32-browser also compiles (2,292 bytes)."
    :utf16 {:as-of "2026-09-02"
            :amu "bb51dc14"
            :oracle "clojure.string/index-of and last-index-of themselves, nil read as -1, on the byte-index-of input set plus the astral cases; asserted equal, and asserted DIFFERENT from byte-index-of where a multi-byte code point precedes the match"
            :executed "a three-module project (clojure.string, kotoba.string, app) compiled to wasm32 (4,426 bytes, exit 0, JVM-free) and instantiated through amu runtime/browser-host.mjs: (utf16-index-of \"a\ud83d\ude00b\" \"b\") = 3, (utf16-last-index-of \"abab\" \"ab\") = 2, (utf16-last-index-of \"a\ud83d\ude00\" \"\") = 3"}}
   :costs
   {:utf16-index-of "the byte-index-of scan, carrying a UTF-16 unit count alongside the byte offset; utf16-last-index-of tries every boundary to the end and keeps the last match, so it never short-circuits"
    :byte-index-of "a naive scan: every code-point boundary of the haystack in turn, and at each one a code-point-by-code-point compare until the needle ends or a pair differs -- O(haystack code points x needle code points) steps worst case, and one string-code-point-at per compared code point. Recursion here is fuel-bounded like any other guest recursion."}
   :hazards
   {:no-contains-precheck "string-contains? would short-circuit an absent needle, but it answers the same question by a second mechanism; if the two ever disagreed this function would return -1 while a match existed. One slower mechanism beats two that must agree."
    :empty-needle "answers 0, matching clojure.string/index-of, and handled before the scan because string-contains? refuses an empty needle."
    :self-synchronising "the scan only looks at code-point boundaries, and that loses nothing: a valid UTF-8 needle begins with an ASCII byte or a lead byte, never a continuation byte, so a byte-level occurrence cannot start inside a code point."
    :minus-one-is-not-nil "utf16-index-of answers -1 where clojure.string/index-of answers nil. A caller who threads the result into arithmetic gets -1 where Clojure would have thrown; the name is the warning, and the option-valued spelling is waiting on :measured :option-return-on-wasm32."
    :not-a-byte-offset "the UTF-16 index is not usable with string-substring or string-code-point-at; (string-substring s 0 (utf16-index-of s x)) is the byte-offset defect in the other direction. byte-index-of is the one that composes."}}}}
